  "transcript": "# Reforming the water sector\n\n## Why privatisation happened and what it changed\n> Privatisation aimed to draw private finance and boost efficiency; it helped deliver major wastewater and drinking-water improvements but left funding and regulation choices that later caused problems.\n\nChair: We’ll start in 1989: why was privatisation pursued and what problem was it meant to solve?\nHugh Goldsmith: The White Paper aimed to benefit customers: more efficiency, innovation and access to private finance. There was also a political aim of share ownership, but the practical goal was better services funded by private money.\nDr David Lloyd Owen: Privatisation itself wasn’t the issue; regulation was. Under the early regulator Ian Byatt there was focus on investment and maintenance. Later reviews shifted to keeping bills low and that squeezed maintenance.\nJohn Abraham: Before 1989 water often lost out for public funding. Private ownership let big investments happen—especially secondary sewage treatment—and drove efficiency in design and construction.\nChair: So outcomes: has water quality and service improved overall since 1989?\nHugh Goldsmith: Yes: major improvements in waste water treatment and big falls in pollutants, driven in part by EU environmental rules; drinking water quality is among the world’s better systems.\n\n\n## Money, debt and the regulator’s role\n> Regulatory accounting (RCV) and debt-financed investment led companies to rely heavily on borrowing; that, plus weak regulatory incentives for maintenance, created vulnerabilities.\n\nChair: There’s a persistent claim that the industry was funded mainly by debt, not equity. How did that happen?\nHugh Goldsmith: Companies started debt-free at privatisation. The RCV model inflates asset values over time and made debt the natural way to fund investment; little fresh equity went in to build assets.\nDr David Lloyd Owen: Rating agencies and Ofwat set expectations. Companies took more debt; servicing it grew costly. Scotland’s public model shows similar debt levels can cost less because of cheaper borrowing.\nChair: Did regulatory choices prioritising low bills undermine maintenance?\nDr David Lloyd Owen: Yes. For 25 years operating and maintenance spend stayed flat despite new assets. That allowed assets to stagnate and left big repair backlogs.\nJohn Abraham: Construction and CapEx delivery are good; the problem is operating and maintaining what we’ve built. England and Wales largely use the same in-house operators, so there's little competitive pressure on O&M.\n\n\n## Different ownership models abroad\n> France, the Netherlands and other countries show many workable models — public ownership, municipal companies, concessions — but success depends on local capacity, finance and practical regulation.\n\nDr Marine Colon: In France local governments own assets and often sign concession contracts with private operators who bill users and are fined under contracts if they fail; local capacity matters more than ownership.\nChair: What are the pros and cons of that French concession model?\nDr Marine Colon: It allows local control and tailored contracts. But France has many tiny utilities that lack the expertise and finance to tackle large climate-driven investments; mergers and river-basin agencies help channel funds.\nProfessor Klaas Schwartz: The Dutch model uses private-law companies owned by municipalities or provinces, enshrined by law. They are autonomous but publicly owned, self-financing and trusted by consumers — but tariffs are rising because major reinvestment is needed.\nHugh Goldsmith: There’s no one-size-fits-all: Scandinavia has municipal systems and public banks to finance investment; Germany keeps assets public and outsources operations; Singapore mixes public ownership with private operators for specific projects.\nJohn Abraham: In practice you can competitively tender operation and maintenance or concessions for catchments; many parts of the world do that well, but England and Wales largely don’t use that market for O&M.\n\n\n## Cunliffe’s reform agenda and the ownership debate\n> Witnesses broadly back Cunliffe’s priorities — clear strategy, stronger regulation and funding — but sharply disagree on whether public ownership is the fastest, cheapest or fairest way to secure investment and better outcomes.\n\nDavid Henderson: Sir Jon Cunliffe’s report is strong: you need Government strategy, a regulator that enforces it, and money (from bills or taxpayers). Ownership alone doesn’t determine outcomes.\nProfessor David Saal: I agree regulation needs reform and more local catchment planning. But that requires changing how Ofwat assesses costs; TotEx and the economic models must reflect local differences.\nProfessor Ewan McGaughey: Ownership matters a great deal. Since privatisation roughly £85bn has been returned to shareholders, and bondholders have taken large sums too. Public ownership lets the state avoid those extra returns and borrow cheaper.\nProfessor David Hall: The major omission from Cunliffe was public ownership options. Other countries put local accountable bodies in charge; here local authorities have almost no power and that damages accountability and trust.\nDavid Henderson: Public ownership is an option but it would require taxpayers to back large sums. There are legal and market risks if the Government forcibly cancels contracts; funding still has to be found.\nProfessor Ewan McGaughey: Special administration can transfer assets without large compensation if shareholders have failed and statutory duties are neglected — that’s a practical route to remove badly performing private owners.\n\n\n## Public expectations, practical reforms and next steps\n> The public overwhelmingly wants greater public control; practical reforms suggested include catchment-based planning, clearer regulation enforced by engineers, tighter dividend and debt rules, and exploring public acquisition where necessary.\n\nTerry Jermy: Public opinion polls show strong support for public ownership. But practically, what changes would deliver better services now?\nProfessor David Hall: Start by restoring local responsibility and accountability: put planning and decision-making at catchment or regional level under public control, as other countries do.\nProfessor David Saal: Strengthen supervision: real engineers and auditors should assess assets and needs company-by-company or catchment-by-catchment instead of relying on opaque econometric models.\nJohn Abraham: Introduce alternative delivery for operation and maintenance: ringfence catchments, competitively tender long-term O&M contracts, use instant penalties for breaches like PFIs do, and bring in global operators.\nProfessor Ewan McGaughey: Tighten rules on dividends and give Ofwat stronger, clearer licence powers; use special administration where owners have failed on pollution and leakage.\nDavid Henderson: All models can work with the three ingredients Cunliffe sets out: clear national strategy, strong enforcement, and reliable funding. The Government must decide the trade-offs and act.\nChair: The Committee will consider these trade-offs: public investment and ownership options, better local planning, and a regulatory model that focuses on real, practical asset needs.\n",
